\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Ezekiel 26:1-6 is another one of those passages which in some sense belong to the section previous, while starting a new section. Read it with chapter 25, and it fits the pattern of those short oracles against various nations all around: because they did thus with respect to Jerusalem in its fall, therefore the LORD takes the following actions, such that they know he is the LORD. Therefore, much of the message from chapter 25 will apply. But it has tight connections to the chapter 26, 27, and 28 judgement on Tyre.\
This raises questions as to why Tyre gets so much more focus than the previous handful of nations. While Tyre says aha over the fall of Jerusalem as Ammon did, they have a wider vision for how they might exploit that fall. This is understandable given their nature as a trade based polity, as is the focus on trade in the subsequent chapters. But perhaps there is more to this focus than just the seaside nature of the polity being judged.\
I am curious what all we might learn about the opening of Ezekiel 26, and I wonder how you see them.
