\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Looking at Ezekiel 26:1-6 in Hebrew, several things strike me immediately:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 The 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1492 \u1462 \u1488 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1 \cf2  echo
\b0 \
Verse 2's 
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1462 \u1488 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1  directly connects to 25:3 (Ammon). But where Ammon's gloating was simple schadenfreude over sanctuary, land, and people, Tyre's statement is strategic calculation: 
\f3 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1491 \u1463 \u1468 \u1500 \u1456 \u1514 \u1445 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1469 \u1506 \u1463 \u1502 \u1460 \u1468 \u1430 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1505 \u1461 \u1443 \u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1464 \u1425 \u1497 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1502 \u1464 \u1468 \u1469 \u1500 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1430 \u1492 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1469 \u1495 \u1459 \u1512 \u1464 \u1469 \u1489 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 "The door of the peoples is broken, it has turned to me, I will be filled \'97 she is laid waste."\
This is commercial analysis, not emotional reaction. Jerusalem was a trade chokepoint (
\f3 \uc0\u1491 \u1463 \u1468 \u1500 \u1456 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  \'97 doors, gates), and Tyre sees its destruction as market opportunity. The verb 
\f3 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1505 \u1461 \u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  (has turned) suggests trade flows redirecting. 
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1469 \u1495 \u1459 \u1512 \u1464 \u1469 \u1489 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  (she is laid waste) almost functions as Tyre's explanation for why it expects to be filled.\

\b The sea imagery inverted
\b0 \
Verse 3's 
\f3 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1469 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1434 \u1497 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1500 \u1463 \u1433 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 \u1433 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1443 \u1501 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1512 \u1463 \u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1428 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1463 \u1469 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1445 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1464 \u1468 \u1430 \u1501 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1490 \u1463 \u1500 \u1464 \u1468 \u1469 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  is devastating: YHWH brings up nations against Tyre "as the sea brings up its waves." The sea was Tyre's domain, her protection, her source of power. Now it becomes the metaphor for her destruction. What should be home becomes threat.\

\b Why extended treatment?
\b0 \
I have a hypothesis beyond the maritime nature: Tyre represents 
\i systematic reduction of sacred realities to commercial transactions
\i0 .\
Ammon gloated emotionally. Moab drew theological conclusions (25:8 \'97 "Judah is like all the nations"). Edom and Philistia took vengeance. But Tyre 
\i calculates profit from destruction of what YHWH established
\i0 . The covenant people become market share. The temple city becomes a broken trade barrier.\
This seems categorically different \'97 not just wrong attitude toward Jerusalem's fall, but a framework that cannot even see what Jerusalem 
\i was
\i0 . The "door of peoples" language treats Israel purely as geography affecting commerce.\

\b The dating puzzle
\b0 \
Verse 1: 
\f3 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1506 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1514 \u1461 \u1468 \u1469 \u1497 \u1470 \u1506 \u1462 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1512 \u1461 \u1445 \u1492 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1504 \u1464 \u1430 \u1492 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1488 \u1462 \u1495 \u1464 \u1443 \u1491 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1495 \u1465 \u1425 \u1491 \u1462 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  \'97 "in the eleventh year, on the first of the month" \'97 but which month? This is unusual for Ezekiel. Is this deliberate ambiguity, textual corruption, or something else?\
What connections do you see to the broader Ezekiel framework that I might be missing?
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
