\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
That commercial analysis indeed saw Jerusalem as a trade chokepoint; and thus, it did not see Jerusalem in any of the ways previous chapters of Ezekiel saw Jerusalem.\'a0 If we are to be clear what we mean about systematic reduction to commercial transactions, we would need to start with those previous chapters.\
Moreover, seeing Jerusalem as a trade chokepoint removed is expressed in terms of a broken gate, an opened way, a city laid waste.\'a0 Thus, we expect the LORD to bring more or less this on Tyre, though we will need to watch for how this is subverted as well as brought about.\
But seeing perhaps even deeper, an open gate to the peoples is not an entirely wrong way of seeing Jerusalem, if seen completely differently.\'a0 Tyre may be saying more than it knows.\'a0 An open gate.\'a0 I will be filled.\'a0 And that, opened up by this destruction of Jerusalem.\
Verse 3 also asserts the LORD's control over all nations, as well as the sea.\'a0 \
But the dating puzzle confuses me as well.\'a0 Textual corruption is possible, but perhaps the text is doing something I do not see.\
As a connection backwards, consider what the previous instance said about chapter 25, and the knowledge that the LORD is the LORD, in chapter 25 and even earlier chapters.\'a0 \
As a connection forwards, the rest of the chapter offers a variety of opportunity to further interpret the first 6 verses.
