\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
While I suggested it myself, I must emphasize that unlike with the contrast between Ezekiel's vision and Tyre's vision of Jerusalem, our speculation on "more than Tyre knows" lacks solid evidence in the book of Ezekiel.\'a0 I am curious whether such evidence can be found; if not, we cannot lend much credence to our speculation.\
On the other hand, I think chapter 26 has excellent support for the universal sovereignty of the LORD.\'a0 The LORD will bring many nations against Tyre, implying control over them, even the most powerful, Babylon.\'a0 Moreover, the outcome is under the LORD's control, for the LORD's purposes.\
You raise the question of multiple fulfillment, and I think that is a possibility.\'a0 But also, the exact failure to take the island is hinted at here; Nebuchadrezzar enters the gates as men enter a city that has been breached.\'a0 Yet Tyre proper is not said to have been breached.\'a0 We know from Babylonian records that Tyre later paid tribute, and its king was captive in Babylon as was the king of Judah.\'a0 We know from Josephus that the siege lasted 13 years, and that afterwards, Babylon appointed the rulers of Tyre.\'a0 We know from later in Ezekiel that Nebuchadrezzar did not receive the military results he wanted from the siege.\
Perhaps all of this together can help explain what verses 8 through 10 are doing, and how they interpret verse 4.\'a0 The killing on the mainland is straightforward enough.\'a0 But siege ramps and battering rams are hard to imagine for the nature of the assault Babylon could bring to bear against an island fortress like Tyre proper - though Alexander's successful assault built a mole so that land siege tactics could be used.\'a0 But siege tactics more generally were certainly used against Tyre as a nation, and seem to have resulted not in breach but in Tyre's surrender and loss of sovereignty.\'a0 It is, I think, this surrender and loss of sovereignty that verse 10's entry as to a breached city refers to.\'a0 And verse 10 still clearly speaks of Nebuchadnezzar.\'a0 But verses 4 and 5, with their interpretation in 12 through 14, may refer to Alexander (as per your one wave among many theory), or they may be a description of loss of sovereignty as the battering rams were a description of siege.
