\f1\b \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Accepting the correction on speculation
\b0 \
You're right. The "more than Tyre knows" reading is theologically suggestive but lacks grounding in Ezekiel's text. I can find no passage in Ezekiel where Jerusalem's destruction explicitly opens access for peoples, or where the "broken gate" language gets redeemed. Without such evidence, the speculation cannot bear interpretive weight. It's the kind of pattern-matching to broader biblical themes that previous instances warned against \'97 importing rather than discovering.\

\b The grammatical shift and the waves
\b0 \
Looking more carefully at the structure:\
Verses 7-11 use singular verbs consistently for Nebuchadnezzar: 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1469 \u1492 \u1458 \u1512 \u1465 \u1425 \u1490 
\f1  (he will kill), 
\f3 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1448 \u1503 
\f1  (he will set), 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1514 \u1461 \u1468 \u1430 \u1503 
\f1  (he will direct), 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1514 \u1465 \u1468 \u1430 \u1509 
\f1  (he will break down), 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1512 \u1456 \u1502 \u1465 \u1430 \u1505 
\f1  (he will trample).\
Verse 12 shifts abruptly to plural: 
\f3 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1513 \u1464 \u1469 \u1473 \u1500 \u1456 \u1500 \u1443 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  (they will plunder), 
\f3 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1464 \u1469 \u1494 \u1456 \u1494 \u1438 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  (they will loot), 
\f3 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1469 \u1512 \u1456 \u1505 \u1493 \u1468 \u1433 
\f1  (they will tear down), 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1514 \u1465 \u1468 \u1425 \u1510 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  (they will break), 
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1469 \u1474 \u1497 \u1502 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  (they will place).\
This grammatical shift seems deliberate. The wave metaphor in verse 3 \'97 
\f3 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1463 \u1469 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1445 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1464 \u1468 \u1430 \u1501 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1490 \u1463 \u1500 \u1464 \u1468 \u1469 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  \'97 prepares for multiple actors. Nebuchadnezzar is named as the first and greatest wave. The plural "they" are subsequent waves that complete what he began.\

\b The "as to a breached city" reading
\b0 \
Your reading of verse 10 is compelling: 
\f3 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1502 \u1456 \u1489 \u1493 \u1465 \u1488 \u1461 \u1430 \u1497 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1506 \u1460 \u1445 \u1497 \u1512 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1489 \u1467 \u1511 \u1464 \u1468 \u1506 \u1464 \u1469 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 "as men enter a city that has been breached." The 
\f3 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 
\f1  makes this explicitly simile. Nebuchadnezzar enters 
\i with the effect of
