\b \cf2 Chapter 27's contribution
\b0 \
The lament in chapter 27 catalogs Tyre's commercial network with almost liturgical comprehensiveness. Every trading partner, every luxury good, every source of wealth \'97 Tarshish with silver and iron, Javan and Tubal with slaves and bronze, Beth-togarmah with horses, Judah and Israel with wheat and honey...\
This serves the same purpose as chapter 16's elaboration of Jerusalem's adorning. The glory is detailed so the loss is undeniable. When 
\f3 \uc0\u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1511 \u1464 \u1468 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  (the east wind) shatters the ship 
\f3 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1500 \u1461 \u1445 \u1489 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1502 \u1460 \u1468 \u1469 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  (in the heart of the seas, 27:26), everything cataloged goes down together.\
But there's something more. The catalog in 27:12-25 places Tyre at the center of a web connecting virtually every known nation. When Tyre falls, the whole system trembles. The coastlands shake (27:35). The merchants hiss (27:36). The interconnection that was Tyre's glory becomes the medium through which YHWH's sovereignty becomes visible to all nations.\

\b The recognition spreading outward
\b0 \
In chapter 25, individual nations faced judgment and recognized YHWH. In chapters 26-27, Tyre's judgment produces recognition not just in Tyre but in all her trading partners. The princes of the sea come down from their thrones (26:16). The coastlands tremble (26:18). The kings are appalled (27:35).\
The commercial network that couldn't see YHWH's sovereignty becomes the transmission medium for recognizing it. What Tyre used to spread goods and influence, YHWH uses to spread knowledge of Himself.\
<notes>This connects to what the previous instance noted about chapter 6 \'97 that even mountains being addressed implies inclusion in moral reality. Here, entire trading networks become the audience for divine self-disclosure. The more comprehensive Tyre's reach, the more comprehensive the recognition when she falls.</notes>\

