The nature of the trade relationships in chapter 27 may assist with interpreting Tyre's terror on her inhabitants.\'a0 Those trade relationships are described somewhat neutrally; they allow a wide network of trade with all its benefits to the parties concerned, but the means used are also exposed.\
Now, it is true that Ezekiel 32 speaks of shame of nations in Sheol, and this has some bearing on Ezekiel 37 and on Ezekiel 26.\'a0 But I do not understand what you are saying about the connection between chapter 26 and 25 or the message from chapter 25.\'a0 This does not mean you are wrong; clarification of your meaning may be sufficient.\
On the other hand, I think you are correct that 'inhabit' is being intentionally repeated in this way.\'a0 Moreover, I think that 'as if you never were' both helps explain some of Ezekiel's language in these verses and the texture of some of the metaphors in earlier chapters; this also rhymes with the way the Psalms and Isaiah enjoin us not to fear man because he fades as if he never were, but to fear God in whose hands all these things are.\
Similarly, I join in your uncertainty that the beauty and life of verse 20 are the evidence we were looking for that Tyre had said more than it knew.\'a0 Perhaps our credence should rise slightly.\'a0 But some read verse 20 not as a first person the LORD placing beauty, but as a second person, borrowing the negative from not being inhabited; that is, they read it as saying that Tyre will not be beautiful in the land of the living.\'a0 Either way, the rhetoric clearly paints this as the LORD's doing, witnessed by the nations in a way that reveals the LORD as the LORD.\
On the other hand, I am not entirely sure that the end of chapter 27 should be read 'and you are not'.\'a0 The grammar also permits 'and you will not be', which is more natural when followed, as here, by 'forever'.\'a0 Quibble about the grammar aside, I agree that both chapter 26 and chapter 27 present the future non-existence of Tyre as a present reality already accomplished by the LORD's speech, and the connection with the chapter 19 lament is apt.
